let __v_194 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"Decide your line before the next ask rather than in the moment, and make it one sentence: what you can do and what you can't. 'I can do Saturday, but not every Saturday' is a thing somebody can plan around, and you don't owe a reason.\",\"Say it once, calmly, and make it about the thing rather than the person: 'I'd rather you asked me first.' One clear line lands where a hint does not, and it is kinder than going quiet about it.\",\"Give them an answer they can work with: what you can do, and what you can't. 'I can help with the school run, but I can't lend money again' is a line somebody can plan around.\"]}")?;
frame.store("ASKED", __v_194.clone())?;
let __v_195 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"Make it one rule rather than one row, said once and warmly the next time they ask: 'of course, if it's back by Sunday.' A rule they agree to is easier to hold than a complaint about the last time.\",\"Ask before the next time rather than after: 'can we agree I'm asked first, even if the answer is going to be yes?' It costs nothing when they would have asked anyway.\",\"Keep the two or three things you actually mind out of reach and let the rest go. Then the one sentence - 'anything of mine, just ask' - covers the lot without a list of every time.\"]}")?;
frame.store("TAKEN", __v_195.clone())?;
let __v_196 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"Give the thing one place to go rather than asking for tidiness in general: 'the shoes live in the cupboard by the door.' A named place is something a person can do; 'clear up after yourself' is not.\",\"Ask for the one job, once, at a calm moment - 'can the dishes go in the dishwasher before bed?' - rather than for a change in character. One job named is one job done.\",\"Say it as the house rule rather than as a complaint about them: 'in this house the boots come off at the door.' A rule is easier to hear than a list of the times they didn't.\"]}")?;
frame.store("MESSY", __v_196.clone())?;
let __v_197 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"Say it once, plainly, as a wish rather than an accusation: 'I'd love to be asked next time, even at short notice.' People often don't know they've left somebody out, and one sentence is usually enough.\",\"Ask for the next one rather than for the reason: 'can I come to the next one?' A straight yes or no tells you more than any hint will, and it is easy for them to say yes to.\",\"Worth saying out loud once - 'I've noticed I don't get asked any more, and it does get to me' - and then letting them answer. The answer is the useful part, whatever it is.\"]}")?;
frame.store("LEFT_OUT", __v_197.clone())?;
let __v_198 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"Say what you would like noticed rather than what they didn't do: 'a thanks when I've done the shopping would go a long way.' People often don't know which bit you want acknowledged, and a thing named is something they can do.\",\"Ask for it once, plainly and without a score kept: 'it would help if you said when it's good.' Then notice it when it comes, because the thanks you asked for is the one they are learning to give.\",\"Worth saying what the effect is, once: 'I do a lot round here and it goes unnoticed, and that is what wears me down.' It is easier to hear than 'you never thank me'.\"]}")?;
frame.store("TAKEN_FOR_GRANTED", __v_198.clone())?;
let __v_199 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"Say it once, about one thing, in the moment: 'please don't comment on that - it isn't helping.' You don't have to justify it, and not liking it is reason enough.\",\"Name the thing and the effect in one sentence - 'when you say that, I stop wanting to tell you things' - and leave it there. It is a statement, not an argument to win.\",\"Answer flatly and warmly the first time, then give the same short answer every time after: 'I heard you, and I'm not changing it.' Repetition is what ends it, not a better argument.\"]}")?;
frame.store("CRITICISED_LINES", __v_199.clone())?;
let __v_200 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"Get it in writing and ask what moved: 'just to confirm, it's Tuesday now - what changed?' A written thread is what you can point at the next time it shifts, and it costs one email.\",\"Ask what moved and what comes off instead of why it moved: 'if this is the new priority, what drops?' That hands the choice to the person who can actually make it.\",\"Raise it once, at the end of a meeting, in plain words: 'when the plan changes twice a week I lose time on the rest.' Say what it costs you, not that it is annoying.\"]}")?;
frame.store("WORK_LINES", __v_200.clone())?;
let __v_201 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"Say what you did, in one sentence, in the room where it comes up: 'I did the analysis behind that.' Credit follows a clear claim, and the quiet hope that somebody notices is what leaves it with them.\",\"Ask for the part you want named rather than the whole thing: 'can the write-up say who did what?' It is a small, reasonable request, and it is much easier to say yes to than a row about taking credit.\",\"Keep a short note of what you did and when - not to send to anyone, but so you can say it plainly the next time it matters. Then say it once, warmly and without the charge: 'that was mine, and I'd like that known.'\"]}")?;
